The user's report that super_spine_router_0 is "swallowing" packets from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is not supported by any live evidence.

A full four-phase broad search was completed. Phase 1 (blind reachability) showed 0% loss in both directions with an average RTT of ~2–3 ms, confirming end-to-end forwarding is working correctly through the entire Clos fabric. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL drop rules on any device, no duplicate MACs, no static ARP poisoning, no addressing or resolver divergence across all 7 nodes. Phase B (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed that all five FRR routers — including super_spine_router_0 — have all BGP sessions in Established state, all daemons (bgpd, zebra, watchfrr) running, no null/blackhole routes injected, and all 16 interfaces using the default fq_codel qdisc with zero flagged shaping. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, no CPU pressure, and no socket exhaustion on any device.

super_spine_router_0 is operating normally: it has two Established BGP sessions (one each to spine_router_0_0 and spine_spine_router_0_1), its FRR stack is fully healthy, and there is no evidence of packet dropping, rate-limiting, or forwarding anomaly. The user's belief that this device is at fault is incorrect. No corrective action is required; the network is functioning as expected.
